---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import pilot from '../content/pilot.json';
import { publicationFor } from '../lib/publication';
import { newQuestions } from '../lib/newQuestions';
const release=publicationFor(pilot.question.id);
const statuses={...pilot.question.statuses,publication:release?.publication_status || pilot.question.statuses.publication};
---
<Base title="Methodology"><article class="article"><p class="eyebrow">METHODS / EVIDENCE AND LIMITS</p><h1>Make the boundaries visible.</h1><h2>A shared process with separate scientific methods</h2><p>Source reading and literature assessment were performed by the agent. Retrieval, quotation checks, protocol freezing, container execution, result export and static-site generation are implemented in code. New questions and resolution claims are not admitted without review.</p>
{newQuestions.length>0 && <><p>The discovery cohort sampled arXiv and Europe PMC across five disciplines. Abstract screening, full-text inspection and independent expert assessment are distinct levels of evidence. Exact quotation checks establish a source match; semantic interpretation and novelty still require assessment.</p><h2>Additional investigations</h2><ul>{newQuestions.filter(q=>q.report_run_id).map(q=><li><a href={'/research/'+q.report_run_id+'/'}>{q.title}</a>: {q.outcome_summary}</li>)}</ul><p>The mathematics pilot uses exact certificates. The streamflow pilot uses a fixed calibration and evaluation split and retains its failed station-support gate. Neither uses the ENSO experiment's forecasting method. Each report records its own protocol, checks and limitations.</p></>}
<h2>Before execution</h2><p>The broker permits only configured HTTPS sources, with bounded retrieval sizes and timeouts. Rights and exact source quotation checks run outside the research container. Plans bind inputs, code, methods, allowed variants, resource budgets and stopping rules before outcomes are inspected. A reporting amendment must preserve the original protocol and identify what was already observed.</p>
<h2>The first ENSO comparison</h2><p>Two fixed ridge regressions predict the anomaly six months ahead from six monthly lags. One adds sine/cosine of the issue month and interactions with the latest anomaly. Persistence is a third, untrained reference. Models train on targets ending in 2014. Holdout issue dates start in 2015. No fitting, tuning or model selection uses holdout outcomes.</p>
<h2>ENSO uncertainty</h2><p>Paired circular calendar-year block resampling uses 2-, 3- and 4-year blocks. The frozen rule requires a 5% RMSE benefit over both comparators and positive lower interval endpoints at every block length. The sample contains few ENSO cycles and the intervals cannot establish future skill.</p>
<h2>Five separate statuses</h2><p>Literature coverage, practical feasibility, research outcome, validation and publication are tracked independently. These are the current first-pilot statuses:</p><dl>{Object.entries(statuses).map(([key,state])=><div><dt>{key}</dt><dd>{state.replaceAll('_',' ')}</dd></div>)}</dl>
<h2>Isolation and validation</h2><p>Research containers have no network, host mounts or publication credentials. They run as unprivileged users with read-only root filesystems. Fresh-container reproduction and separate implementations check repeatability and calculations. Methodological adequacy and external scientific review remain separate questions. Readiness scores describe whether a bounded job can be attempted; they are not probabilities of correctness or novelty.</p>
<h2>Public disclosure gate</h2><p>Publication follows human approval of each exact disclosure bundle. Frozen scientific files retain their approved hashes; current publication and review metadata are recorded separately. New scientific content requires approval before a public PR or upload. Implementation maintenance is reviewed through pull requests and preserves the frozen experiments.</p><p><a href="/data/v1/plan.json">Inspect the first frozen protocol ↗</a></p></article></Base>
